WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.509
<v Ava>Welcome back to the deep dive. I'm Ava, and this is Theme 7: problem solving and creativity.

2
00:00:06.849 --> 00:00:13.580
<v Leo>And I'm Leo. We've rebuilt this episode, so each topic now goes in the same order: first the logic,

3
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.135
<v Leo>then the studies, then a conclusion, and then the Canvas questions.

4
00:00:18.475 --> 00:00:26.261
<v Ava>Let's start with a little story from the reading. Ten minutes before a presentation, a student notices that the hem of her skirt has come loose.

5
00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:28.820
<v Leo>No needle, and only ten minutes.

6
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.488
<v Ava>And she wants to get to a fixed hem before she walks on stage.

7
00:00:32.688 --> 00:00:35.483
<v Ava>She runs into her professor's office, and staples it.

8
00:00:35.823 --> 00:00:41.241
<v Leo>And this whole theme asks how she did that, and why, sometimes, we get stuck on the way.

9
00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:44.696
<v Leo>So how does each topic work?

10
00:00:45.036 --> 00:00:49.943
<v Ava>Step one is the logic. What's the idea in plain words, and why does it make sense?

11
00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:55.369
<v Ava>Step two is the evidence. Which studies support the idea, and which ones push back on it?

12
00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:59.516
<v Ava>Step three is the verdict: what you should take away, including the limits.

13
00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:03.471
<v Ava>And step four is the Canvas questions for that topic, worked out.

14
00:01:03.811 --> 00:01:05.325
<v Leo>And the three blocks?

15
00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:11.809
<v Ava>Block A is the toolkit: how you represent a problem, and the strategies you use when there is a clear path.

16
00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:16.809
<v Ava>Block B takes the clear path away: insight, and the things that block us or help us.

17
00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:23.281
<v Ava>And Block C is one experiment that asks a surprising question: do you need your mental brakes to be creative?

18
00:01:24.581 --> 00:01:27.205
<v Leo>Okay, A point one. What's the essence?

19
00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:33.688
<v Ava>The essence is that every problem is a gap. You start in an initial state, you want to reach a goal state,

20
00:01:33.688 --> 00:01:35.716
<v Ava>and there are obstacles in between.

21
00:01:35.916 --> 00:01:41.662
<v Ava>And here's the key logic of this whole lecture: before you can cross the gap, you have to see it.

22
00:01:41.662 --> 00:01:47.526
<v Ava>You build a mental representation of the problem, an inner model of what's given and what's wanted.

23
00:01:47.526 --> 00:01:51.553
<v Ava>That can be symbols, a matrix, a diagram, or a picture in your head.

24
00:01:51.893 --> 00:01:53.856
<v Leo>And attention is part of that.

25
00:01:54.196 --> 00:02:01.727
<v Ava>Yes, for three reasons. Attention is limited, competing thoughts divide it, and you have to aim it at the right part of the problem.

26
00:02:01.927 --> 00:02:08.151
<v Ava>And then two kinds of cues shape your representation: cues from the setting, that's situated cognition,

27
00:02:08.151 --> 00:02:15.644
<v Ava>and cues from your own body, which is embodied cognition. Hold on to that body idea, because the first studies are about it.

28
00:02:16.944 --> 00:02:20.784
<v Leo>Once I can see the problem, I have to choose how to attack it.

29
00:02:21.124 --> 00:02:23.983
<v Ava>Right. Look at these fourteen letters. It's an anagram.

30
00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:29.713
<v Ava>One option is an algorithm: a method that will always produce a solution, but can be slow.

31
00:02:29.713 --> 00:02:38.376
<v Ava>The extreme version is exhaustive search: try every possible order. With fourteen letters that's more than eighty-seven billion arrangements.

32
00:02:38.376 --> 00:02:44.705
<v Ava>You'd find the word simultaneously, but, as Matlin and Farmer put it, you might grow a few years older.

33
00:02:45.045 --> 00:02:47.285
<v Leo>So the other option is the shortcut.

34
00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:56.389
<v Ava>A heuristic: a general rule that is usually correct. You only try pronounceable pairs that often start English words.

35
00:02:56.389 --> 00:03:01.257
<v Ava>Reject L S, L T, L Y. Try L E, L U, and ideally S T. Much faster.

36
00:03:01.597 --> 00:03:02.899
<v Leo>But there's a catch.

37
00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:10.030
<v Ava>There's always a catch. Take these letters, which spell lymphoid. If your rule says reject rare starting pairs,

38
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:16.821
<v Ava>you throw away L Y, and you will never find the answer. So that's the logic: an algorithm buys you a guarantee,

39
00:03:16.821 --> 00:03:19.452
<v Ava>a heuristic trades the guarantee for speed.

40
00:03:19.792 --> 00:03:24.464
<v Leo>Algorithm: always, but slowly. Heuristic: usually, but quickly.

41
00:03:25.764 --> 00:03:33.528
<v Leo>Now, Theme 6 gave us System 1 and System 2. So heuristics are System 1 and algorithms are System 2,

42
00:03:33.528 --> 00:03:33.999
<v Leo>right?

43
00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:39.331
<v Ava>That's the tempting idea, and Robinson-Riegler says it's too neat. Both can be either.

44
00:03:39.531 --> 00:03:44.864
<v Ava>Your multiplication tables are an algorithm, but so well practised that they're System 1.

45
00:03:45.064 --> 00:03:50.952
<v Ava>Following a brand-new recipe closely is an algorithm too, but slow and deliberate: System 2.

46
00:03:51.152 --> 00:03:56.699
<v Ava>A heuristic can be fast: you glance at a blackjack hand on nineteen and don't take another card.

47
00:03:56.899 --> 00:04:01.635
<v Ava>Or slow: solving a problem through an analogy is deliberate System 2 work.

48
00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.340
<v Leo>So which is better?

49
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.427
<v Ava>Given our limited capacity and limited information, heuristics serve us best most of the time.

50
00:04:09.427 --> 00:04:16.459
<v Ava>And neither system is better in general. It depends on the situation, which is exactly what the evidence will test.

51
00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:20.511
<v Leo>Okay, the evidence. Start with the body.

52
00:04:20.851 --> 00:04:26.178
<v Ava>Here's the classic task. Two ropes hang from the ceiling, too far apart to hold both at once,

53
00:04:26.178 --> 00:04:28.125
<v Ava>and you have to tie them together.

54
00:04:28.465 --> 00:04:31.729
<v Leo>And the solution is to make one rope swing toward you.

55
00:04:32.069 --> 00:04:37.676
<v Ava>Right. Thomas and Lleras gave people an exercise break, and some were told to swing their arms.

56
00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:44.464
<v Ava>Those people were more likely to solve it. The movement suggested the solution before they could put it into words.

57
00:04:44.664 --> 00:04:51.064
<v Ava>Chu and Kita found that people are more accurate on mental rotation tasks when they're allowed to move their hands.

58
00:04:51.264 --> 00:05:01.504
<v Ava>And Alibali and colleagues found that people with earlier experience of hand gestures were more likely to choose a hand-movement strategy for a problem about moving gears.

59
00:05:01.844 --> 00:05:06.665
<v Leo>So all three support the idea that your body helps you represent a problem.

60
00:05:07.005 --> 00:05:12.418
<v Ava>Yes. And remember this rope task, because Sternberg uses the very same problem in Block B,

61
00:05:12.418 --> 00:05:13.981
<v Ava>but for a different point.

62
00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:20.081
<v Leo>And the second set of evidence is about intuition versus analysis.

63
00:05:20.421 --> 00:05:27.428
<v Ava>Pretz, 2008. Students rated possible actions in everyday college dilemmas. In one, you need a recommendation letter,

64
00:05:27.428 --> 00:05:34.737
<v Ava>and you bump into your professor in a coffee shop with what seem to be his kids. Some students got analytic instructions,

65
00:05:34.737 --> 00:05:40.234
<v Ava>a four-step method. Some got intuitive instructions: imagine it vividly and trust your gut.

66
00:05:40.234 --> 00:05:46.576
<v Ava>And some got no instructions. The score is how far you are from the consensus answer, so lower is better.

67
00:05:46.776 --> 00:05:55.544
<v Ava>First-years did best with intuition, about nine point nine, against about ten point nine with analysis and ten point eight with no instructions.

68
00:05:55.744 --> 00:06:01.467
<v Ava>Juniors flipped. They did best with analysis, about nine point seven, and worst with intuition,

69
00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:02.251
<v Ava>about twelve.

70
00:06:02.591 --> 00:06:07.220
<v Leo>So the gut works for beginners, and analysis works for experienced people.

71
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:15.175
<v Ava>Because analysis needs knowledge to work with. For a newcomer, the problem is ill defined and analysis just overwhelms them.

72
00:06:15.175 --> 00:06:21.255
<v Ava>For an experienced student, knowledge makes the problem look well defined, and analysis can use it.

73
00:06:21.255 --> 00:06:26.291
<v Ava>So this study supports the logic we just saw: neither system is better in general.

74
00:06:27.591 --> 00:06:29.489
<v Leo>So, verdict on A point one?

75
00:06:29.829 --> 00:06:36.635
<v Ava>One: a problem is a gap, and seeing it clearly comes first. The embodiment studies show your body can help you see it.

76
00:06:36.835 --> 00:06:42.820
<v Ava>Two: you then choose between an algorithm, which is guaranteed but possibly slow, and a heuristic,

77
00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:46.179
<v Ava>which is fast and usually right, but with no guarantee.

78
00:06:46.379 --> 00:06:51.535
<v Ava>Three: don't equate heuristics with gut thinking. Both kinds of strategy can be fast or slow,

79
00:06:51.535 --> 00:06:56.192
<v Ava>and Pretz shows that whether you should trust your gut depends on how much you know.

80
00:06:56.532 --> 00:07:01.204
<v Leo>Algorithm: always, but slowly. Heuristic: usually, but quickly.

81
00:07:02.504 --> 00:07:12.484
<v Leo>Now the Canvas question for this topic. A point two: a video shows an algorithm that solves the Tower of Hanoi in the minimum number of moves.

82
00:07:12.484 --> 00:07:15.155
<v Leo>Why is an algorithm so efficient here?

83
00:07:15.495 --> 00:07:21.006
<v Ava>Start from why algorithms are usually slow. An exhaustive search tries every possibility.

84
00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:26.332
<v Ava>It wanders through every branch, like the eighty-seven billion orders of that anagram.

85
00:07:26.532 --> 00:07:32.601
<v Ava>But the Tower of Hanoi is well structured. The start, the goal and the legal moves are completely fixed,

86
00:07:32.601 --> 00:07:38.437
<v Ava>and the problem has a regular pattern: the four-disk problem always contains the three-disk problem.

87
00:07:38.437 --> 00:07:44.215
<v Ava>So the algorithm doesn't need to search at all. It follows a fixed rule that matches the structure,

88
00:07:44.215 --> 00:07:48.825
<v Ava>with no dead ends, and gets there in the minimum: fifteen moves for four disks.

89
00:07:49.165 --> 00:07:52.749
<v Leo>So it keeps the guarantee and loses the usual cost.

90
00:07:53.089 --> 00:07:58.509
<v Ava>Exactly. And note that this only works because the problem is well structured. In Block B,

91
00:07:58.509 --> 00:08:03.628
<v Ava>with ill-structured problems, there is no clear structure for an algorithm to follow.

92
00:08:04.928 --> 00:08:08.448
<v Leo>A point two, the analogy approach. What's the logic?

93
00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:12.543
<v Ava>Very simple. You already solved a problem once. That's the source.

94
00:08:12.743 --> 00:08:20.593
<v Ava>A new problem comes along, the target, and you reuse the old solution. Like looking back at a worked example in your statistics book.

95
00:08:20.793 --> 00:08:26.425
<v Ava>The Wright brothers did this. Birds control their flight with small adjustments of their wing tips,

96
00:08:26.425 --> 00:08:30.692
<v Ava>so they built wing tips that pilots could adjust with metal rods and gears.

97
00:08:31.032 --> 00:08:35.640
<v Leo>And Robinson-Riegler calls it potentially the most powerful heuristic.

98
00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:40.353
<v Ava>Yes. But the whole lecture is really about why people so often fail to use it.

99
00:08:41.653 --> 00:08:43.488
<v Leo>Give me the classic example.

100
00:08:43.828 --> 00:08:49.311
<v Ava>Duncker's radiation problem. A doctor has to destroy a stomach tumour without operating.

101
00:08:49.511 --> 00:08:55.389
<v Ava>One ray strong enough to destroy the tumour also destroys all the healthy tissue it passes through.

102
00:08:55.389 --> 00:08:58.001
<v Ava>Weak rays are harmless, but they do nothing.

103
00:08:58.201 --> 00:09:03.525
<v Ava>The solution is convergence. Send many weak rays from different angles. Only the tumour,

104
00:09:03.525 --> 00:09:05.945
<v Ava>where they all meet, gets the full dose.

105
00:09:06.285 --> 00:09:08.056
<v Leo>And the military story?

106
00:09:08.396 --> 00:09:14.368
<v Ava>A general must capture a fortress. The roads to it are mined, so a big army would set off the mines,

107
00:09:14.368 --> 00:09:21.355
<v Ava>but small groups can pass. So he sends small groups along many roads at once. Look how the parts map onto each other:

108
00:09:21.355 --> 00:09:28.343
<v Ava>the tumour is the fortress, the rays are the army, the weak rays from many angles are the small groups on many roads.

109
00:09:28.543 --> 00:09:34.273
<v Ava>On the surface they're totally different, cancer versus a war. But the structure is the same.

110
00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:39.572
<v Ava>That shared structure is called a schema: many weak forces, along many paths, at once.

111
00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:48.424
<v Leo>So the essence is: surface features are the objects and words, structural features are the relationships that matter for the solution.

112
00:09:48.764 --> 00:09:51.132
<v Ava>Exactly, and we tend to look at the wrong one.

113
00:09:52.432 --> 00:09:54.587
<v Leo>So why do people miss analogies?

114
00:09:54.927 --> 00:10:00.687
<v Ava>Novick and Holyoak describe three steps. First you have to notice that two problems are related.

115
00:10:00.887 --> 00:10:02.785
<v Ava>Then you map the parts onto each other.

116
00:10:02.985 --> 00:10:05.524
<v Ava>And then you build the general schema and apply it.

117
00:10:05.724 --> 00:10:11.217
<v Ava>And research shows the big culprit is step one, noticing. It's basically a memory failure:

118
00:10:11.217 --> 00:10:16.892
<v Ava>the new problem doesn't remind you of the old one, because it looks different on the surface.

119
00:10:16.892 --> 00:10:22.812
<v Ava>A hint fixes it, because a hint is a retrieval cue. But in everyday life, nobody gives you hints.

120
00:10:23.152 --> 00:10:25.456
<v Leo>And there's a nice real-world case.

121
00:10:25.796 --> 00:10:33.846
<v Ava>Rutgers University wanted a system so applicants could track their applications. Staff first copied other universities' systems,

122
00:10:33.846 --> 00:10:39.506
<v Ava>similar on the surface, and that failed. Then they realised the real problem was tracking,

123
00:10:39.506 --> 00:10:42.713
<v Ava>studied how FedEx tracks packages, and that worked.

124
00:10:44.013 --> 00:10:47.917
<v Leo>Okay, the evidence. Do people actually use analogies?

125
00:10:48.257 --> 00:10:54.977
<v Ava>Gick and Holyoak gave people the radiation problem. With nothing else, ten percent found the convergence solution.

126
00:10:55.177 --> 00:11:01.279
<v Ava>When people had memorised the military story first, as a story-comprehension task, thirty percent did.

127
00:11:01.619 --> 00:11:05.352
<v Leo>So only about twenty percent spotted the link by themselves.

128
00:11:05.692 --> 00:11:13.756
<v Ava>That supports the noticing problem. As Robinson-Riegler puts it, people use an analogy only when they're practically hit over the head with it.

129
00:11:13.956 --> 00:11:17.135
<v Ava>So in 1983 they asked: can we boost it?

130
00:11:18.435 --> 00:11:24.599
<v Ava>Each extra was compared with a one-analogy condition. First, a statement of the general principle:

131
00:11:24.599 --> 00:11:28.184
<v Ava>twenty-nine versus thirty-two percent. Basically nothing.

132
00:11:28.384 --> 00:11:33.739
<v Ava>A diagram of converging arrows: forty percent without, twenty-two with. No help either.

133
00:11:33.939 --> 00:11:39.341
<v Ava>But adding a second analogous story, and asking people to find what the two had in common:

134
00:11:39.341 --> 00:11:41.683
<v Ava>twenty-one percent jumped to fifty-two.

135
00:11:42.023 --> 00:11:44.540
<v Leo>So comparing two cases is what works.

136
00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:51.067
<v Ava>Yes. Comparing forces you to map the stories and pull out the shared principle. They call it schema induction.

137
00:11:52.367 --> 00:11:55.716
<v Leo>Does talking through a problem help you find analogies?

138
00:11:56.056 --> 00:12:02.337
<v Ava>Lane and Schooler tested that. People read sixteen short scenarios, and later, for eight new scenarios,

139
00:12:02.337 --> 00:12:09.411
<v Ava>picked which original was most analogous. Each new one matched one original on the surface and another on structure.

140
00:12:09.611 --> 00:12:14.645
<v Ava>People who thought aloud chose three point eight surface matches and only one structural.

141
00:12:14.845 --> 00:12:19.027
<v Ava>Silent people: one point nine surface, two point three structural.

142
00:12:19.367 --> 00:12:20.817
<v Leo>So talking flipped it.

143
00:12:21.157 --> 00:12:27.707
<v Ava>Because surface features are easier to put into words. Verbalising pushes you toward what is easy to verbalise.

144
00:12:29.007 --> 00:12:31.204
<v Leo>But is the picture really that bleak?

145
00:12:31.544 --> 00:12:38.171
<v Ava>Maybe partly a lab artefact. Blanchette and Dunbar had people produce their own analogies for a problem,

146
00:12:38.171 --> 00:12:41.357
<v Ava>and those analogies shared structure, not surface.

147
00:12:41.557 --> 00:12:49.272
<v Ava>And Markman, Taylor and Gentner noticed that lab problems are nearly always written. So they had people either listen to proverbs,

148
00:12:49.272 --> 00:12:56.986
<v Ava>like the swiftest steed can stumble, or read them. With structural cues, people remembered the original better if they'd heard it.

149
00:12:56.986 --> 00:12:58.944
<v Ava>With surface cues, no difference.

150
00:12:59.144 --> 00:13:03.389
<v Ava>And Catrambone and colleagues tried acting things out. Let's look at that one.

151
00:13:04.689 --> 00:13:09.764
<v Ava>Everyone read the fortress story. Then they recalled it in words, or while drawing a sketch,

152
00:13:09.764 --> 00:13:12.412
<v Ava>or while moving blocks to act out what happened.

153
00:13:12.612 --> 00:13:19.187
<v Ava>Then the radiation problem, with no hint. Acting it out: point five two. Sketching: point two seven.

154
00:13:19.187 --> 00:13:20.633
<v Ava>Words: point one nine.

155
00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:26.465
<v Ava>Then they were told to use the story. And almost everyone got it: point eight five to point nine one.

156
00:13:26.805 --> 00:13:31.264
<v Leo>So applying the analogy is easy. Noticing it is the hard part.

157
00:13:31.604 --> 00:13:37.321
<v Ava>And acting it out helped people notice. It's embodiment again, like the arm swinging in A point one.

158
00:13:38.621 --> 00:13:40.157
<v Leo>Verdict on analogies?

159
00:13:40.497 --> 00:13:46.087
<v Ava>Analogy means reusing an old solution, and it works when you look past the surface to the structure.

160
00:13:46.287 --> 00:13:51.449
<v Ava>Most failures happen at noticing. That's a memory problem, not a problem of applying.

161
00:13:51.649 --> 00:13:57.459
<v Ava>What helps: comparing two analogues, acting it out, a hint, and maybe listening rather than reading,

162
00:13:57.459 --> 00:13:59.201
<v Ava>and making your own analogies.

163
00:13:59.401 --> 00:14:03.391
<v Ava>What doesn't: just stating the principle, a diagram, or thinking aloud.

164
00:14:03.731 --> 00:14:07.037
<v Leo>Notice, map, schema. Noticing is where it breaks.

165
00:14:08.337 --> 00:14:17.258
<v Leo>Canvas question A point six. Thinking aloud pushed people to surface similarities, but spoken presentation helped structural recall.

166
00:14:17.258 --> 00:14:19.217
<v Leo>Why aren't these in conflict?

167
00:14:19.557 --> 00:14:26.094
<v Ava>Because the word verbal means something different in each. In Lane and Schooler, the participant produces speech.

168
00:14:26.094 --> 00:14:29.392
<v Ava>In Markman, the participant receives speech: they listen.

169
00:14:29.592 --> 00:14:35.234
<v Ava>And the stage differs. Lane and Schooler's people talked while retrieving, choosing an analogue.

170
00:14:35.234 --> 00:14:38.936
<v Ava>Markman's people listened while encoding, learning the proverb.

171
00:14:39.136 --> 00:14:45.188
<v Ava>So attention goes to different things. Speaking draws attention to what is easy to put into words,

172
00:14:45.188 --> 00:14:51.363
<v Ava>the surface. Listening helps people follow relations in language, the structure. Both studies agree:

173
00:14:51.363 --> 00:14:57.291
<v Ava>what matters is whether the verbal activity points attention at the surface or at the structure.

174
00:14:58.591 --> 00:15:03.775
<v Leo>And A point seven: how do Catrambone's results replicate Gick and Holyoak?

175
00:15:04.115 --> 00:15:09.653
<v Ava>First pairing: the sketch in phase two is like the diagram. Sketching gave point two seven,

176
00:15:09.653 --> 00:15:15.130
<v Ava>barely above words. And Gick and Holyoak's diagram didn't help either, twenty-two percent.

177
00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:18.173
<v Ava>A picture of the solution doesn't make you notice.

178
00:15:18.373 --> 00:15:25.452
<v Ava>Second pairing: phase three, the hint, is like the second analogue. With a hint, point eight five to point nine one.

179
00:15:25.452 --> 00:15:31.493
<v Ava>With a second analogue, fifty-two percent. In both, the link between the problems is made explicit.

180
00:15:31.693 --> 00:15:37.654
<v Ava>And what it shows: the main difficulty is noticing, not applying. Once you have a retrieval cue,

181
00:15:37.654 --> 00:15:46.285
<v Ava>mapping and applying are easy. One fine point for the exam: a hint helps retrieval, while comparing two analogues helps you build a schema.

182
00:15:47.585 --> 00:15:50.913
<v Leo>A point three. Means-ends analysis. What's the essence?

183
00:15:51.253 --> 00:15:53.835
<v Ava>Back to the loose hem. The goal is a fixed hem.

184
00:15:54.035 --> 00:16:01.629
<v Ava>She split that into two smaller problems. First: find an object that can fix a hem, even if it's not a usual hem-fixer.

185
00:16:01.829 --> 00:16:03.984
<v Ava>Second: find where such an object is.

186
00:16:04.184 --> 00:16:06.829
<v Ava>A stapler in her professor's office solved both.

187
00:16:07.029 --> 00:16:16.291
<v Ava>So means-ends has two parts. You divide the problem into subproblems, and for each one you reduce the difference between where you are and where you want to be.

188
00:16:16.291 --> 00:16:23.989
<v Ava>You identify the ends you want, then the means to get there. Researchers see it as one of the most effective and flexible strategies.

189
00:16:25.289 --> 00:16:27.103
<v Leo>Show me with the Tower of Hanoi.

190
00:16:27.443 --> 00:16:33.454
<v Ava>Four disks start on the left rod. The goal is to get them all onto the right rod, moving one at a time,

191
00:16:33.454 --> 00:16:35.613
<v Ava>never a bigger disk on a smaller one.

192
00:16:35.813 --> 00:16:40.846
<v Ava>Ask: what's the biggest difference from the goal? The largest disk isn't on the right rod.

193
00:16:40.846 --> 00:16:46.493
<v Ava>And it can only move when the three disks on top of it are out of the way. So that becomes a subgoal:

194
00:16:46.493 --> 00:16:51.749
<v Ava>move the top three to the middle. That takes seven moves, and you split it again the same way.

195
00:16:51.949 --> 00:16:54.659
<v Ava>Then move the largest disk across: one move.

196
00:16:54.859 --> 00:17:01.207
<v Ava>And now you have the same problem, one disk smaller: move the three onto the big one, seven more moves.

197
00:17:01.207 --> 00:17:04.288
<v Ava>Seven plus one plus seven is fifteen, the minimum.

198
00:17:04.628 --> 00:17:10.196
<v Leo>And along the way you sometimes put a small disk on the goal rod and take it off again.

199
00:17:10.536 --> 00:17:13.843
<v Ava>Yes, a move that looks like going backward. Hold on to that.

200
00:17:15.143 --> 00:17:16.508
<v Leo>And hill climbing?

201
00:17:16.848 --> 00:17:22.695
<v Ava>Imagine hiking toward the top of a hill in unfamiliar country. At every fork, you can't see far,

202
00:17:22.695 --> 00:17:27.932
<v Ava>so you take the path that goes most steeply up. That's the rule: at each choice point,

203
00:17:27.932 --> 00:17:31.525
<v Ava>pick the step that seems to lead most directly to the goal.

204
00:17:31.725 --> 00:17:36.800
<v Ava>It's quick, and useful when you can only see the next step. But you can end up on a small hill,

205
00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:38.829
<v Ava>where every step from there goes down.

206
00:17:39.029 --> 00:17:44.304
<v Ava>To reach the real top, you'd first have to go down, and hill climbing never does that.

207
00:17:44.304 --> 00:17:49.640
<v Ava>Matlin's example: a student who wants a high salary takes a job straight after college,

208
00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:53.749
<v Ava>although a graduate degree would probably pay more in the long run.

209
00:17:54.089 --> 00:17:58.676
<v Leo>So hill climbing favours short-term goals over long-term solutions.

210
00:17:59.976 --> 00:18:04.051
<v Leo>These two sound similar. Both reduce the distance to the goal.

211
00:18:04.391 --> 00:18:11.367
<v Ava>That's the trap. Means-ends looks at the whole route and divides it into subgoals. Hill climbing only looks at the next step.

212
00:18:11.567 --> 00:18:17.817
<v Ava>So means-ends can plan a temporary step back if a subgoal needs it, even if people dislike doing that.

213
00:18:17.817 --> 00:18:19.716
<v Ava>Hill climbing never steps back.

214
00:18:19.916 --> 00:18:24.652
<v Ava>Means-ends costs more planning. Hill climbing risks getting stuck on a local peak.

215
00:18:24.852 --> 00:18:27.305
<v Ava>And both are heuristics: no guarantee.

216
00:18:27.645 --> 00:18:32.040
<v Leo>Means-ends plans the route. Hill climbing only looks at the next step.

217
00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:36.519
<v Leo>Is there evidence that people really think in subgoals?

218
00:18:36.859 --> 00:18:44.823
<v Ava>Yes. Greeno, 1974, used the Elves-and-Goblins problem. People paused at exactly the points where a new subproblem began,

219
00:18:44.823 --> 00:18:52.389
<v Ava>because they had to plan the next sequence of moves. And working memory is especially active during that planning.

220
00:18:52.589 --> 00:18:58.584
<v Ava>But there's a human limit. People are reluctant to move away from the goal, even when the solution needs it.

221
00:18:58.784 --> 00:19:04.322
<v Ava>In Elves and Goblins, three Elves and three Goblins have to cross a river in a boat that holds two,

222
00:19:04.322 --> 00:19:10.866
<v Ava>and there may never be more Goblins than Elves on a bank. There's a crucial step where two creatures have to go back.

223
00:19:10.866 --> 00:19:15.061
<v Ava>If you only ever move creatures toward the goal bank, you'll never find it.

224
00:19:16.361 --> 00:19:18.345
<v Leo>And then there's the computer part.

225
00:19:18.685 --> 00:19:26.045
<v Ava>How would you test that people really use means-ends? Newell and Simon first asked people to talk aloud while solving problems.

226
00:19:26.245 --> 00:19:29.040
<v Ava>Then they wrote a program that follows the same steps.

227
00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:35.981
<v Ava>And then you compare. The program should be neither better nor worse than a person. It should even make the same false starts.

228
00:19:36.181 --> 00:19:43.157
<v Ava>Their program was the General Problem Solver, 1972, and its basic strategy was means-ends analysis.

229
00:19:43.157 --> 00:19:48.512
<v Ava>It was the first program to simulate a variety of human symbolic behaviours.

230
00:19:48.712 --> 00:19:55.752
<v Ava>But they eventually discarded it, because it wasn't as general as they wanted. Real-life problems aren't that clear-cut.

231
00:19:55.952 --> 00:20:03.227
<v Ava>Later, John Anderson's simulations, linked to his ACT-R theory, ended up as cognitive tutors for high-school maths.

232
00:20:04.527 --> 00:20:05.977
<v Leo>And the culture study.

233
00:20:06.317 --> 00:20:12.206
<v Ava>Güss, Tuason and Gerhard, 2010. About a hundred students each from the US, the Philippines,

234
00:20:12.206 --> 00:20:18.742
<v Ava>Germany, Brazil and India. For about ninety minutes they thought aloud while working in a microworld,

235
00:20:18.742 --> 00:20:24.890
<v Ava>a computer simulation of a complex problem, like commanding a fire brigade during forest fires.

236
00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:27.608
<v Ava>The researchers counted planning comments.

237
00:20:27.808 --> 00:20:34.030
<v Ava>German students made the most planning comments. Americans, Brazilians and Indians were in the middle,

238
00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:39.093
<v Ava>and Filipinos made the fewest. Your excerpt gives the order, not the exact numbers.

239
00:20:39.293 --> 00:20:45.813
<v Ava>The hypothesis was that countries with more economic resources would plan more. It was only partly supported,

240
00:20:45.813 --> 00:20:48.744
<v Ava>because the Americans planned less than expected.

241
00:20:48.944 --> 00:20:55.119
<v Ava>And the key exam point: a group difference isn't a cause. Groups differ in many ways besides country,

242
00:20:55.119 --> 00:20:58.053
<v Ava>so the study can't give a clear-cut explanation.

243
00:20:59.353 --> 00:21:00.953
<v Leo>Verdict on A point three?

244
00:21:01.293 --> 00:21:07.907
<v Ava>Means-ends: split the problem into subgoals and close each gap. Greeno's pauses show people really do this.

245
00:21:08.107 --> 00:21:11.627
<v Ava>The best route sometimes means a step back, and people hate that.

246
00:21:11.827 --> 00:21:17.672
<v Ava>Hill climbing: always take the step that looks like progress. Quick, but you can stop on a local peak.

247
00:21:17.872 --> 00:21:24.734
<v Ava>And computer simulation is a way to test whether a theory of thinking really works. Culture may shape planning too,

248
00:21:24.734 --> 00:21:26.405
<v Ava>but be careful about causes.

249
00:21:26.745 --> 00:21:31.140
<v Leo>Means-ends plans the route. Hill climbing only looks at the next step.

250
00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:39.544
<v Leo>Canvas A point one: discuss the steps of means-ends analysis, using the four-disk Tower of Hanoi.

251
00:21:39.884 --> 00:21:44.940
<v Ava>Step one: state the start and the goal. Four disks on the left, and you want them on the right.

252
00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:49.833
<v Ava>Step two: find the biggest difference. The largest disk isn't on the right rod.

253
00:21:50.033 --> 00:21:56.693
<v Ava>Step three: set a subgoal that removes the obstacle. The big disk can only move when the three on top are elsewhere,

254
00:21:56.693 --> 00:22:03.409
<v Ava>so move them to the middle, seven moves, and split that subgoal again until you reach a move you can make right away.

255
00:22:03.609 --> 00:22:10.036
<v Ava>Step four: make the move and compare again. Largest disk across, one move, then the three on top of it,

256
00:22:10.036 --> 00:22:11.908
<v Ava>seven moves. Fifteen in total.

257
00:22:12.108 --> 00:22:17.030
<v Ava>And add the research: Greeno found that people pause when they start a new subproblem,

258
00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:24.012
<v Ava>and people dislike moves that look like going backward, like putting a small disk on the goal rod and taking it off again.

259
00:22:25.312 --> 00:22:32.155
<v Leo>A point three: the problem-space metaphor compares problem solving to finding a way through a maze.

260
00:22:32.155 --> 00:22:33.952
<v Leo>Which heuristic fits best?

261
00:22:34.292 --> 00:22:43.956
<v Ava>Means-ends. You have a start, a goal, and you search for steps that close the gap. That's exactly Newell and Simon's idea of subgoals and reducing the difference.

262
00:22:44.156 --> 00:22:49.171
<v Ava>Why not hill climbing? In a maze, the right path often first leads away from the exit.

263
00:22:49.171 --> 00:22:54.012
<v Ava>Hill climbing always heads straight toward the goal, so it gets stuck in dead ends.

264
00:22:54.352 --> 00:22:58.085
<v Leo>And A point four: why is hill climbing called a heuristic?

265
00:22:58.425 --> 00:23:03.506
<v Ava>Because it has both features of a heuristic. It's a rule of thumb that is usually right:

266
00:23:03.506 --> 00:23:06.681
<v Ava>pick the step that looks most directly toward the goal.

267
00:23:06.881 --> 00:23:15.042
<v Ava>And it gives no guarantee. You only look one step ahead, so a path can end abruptly. An algorithm would systematically try all routes,

268
00:23:15.042 --> 00:23:16.929
<v Ava>which is sure, but much slower.

269
00:23:18.229 --> 00:23:23.925
<v Leo>And A point five. What would means-ends versus hill climbing look like for passing this course?

270
00:23:24.265 --> 00:23:30.883
<v Ava>Means-ends: the goal is passing the exam. Find the biggest gaps between what you know and what the exam asks,

271
00:23:30.883 --> 00:23:37.137
<v Ava>and turn each into a subgoal. Know each theme's models and studies, be able to apply them to new cases,

272
00:23:37.137 --> 00:23:43.026
<v Ava>practise exam-style questions. Plan backward from the exam date, and accept steps that feel slow,

273
00:23:43.026 --> 00:23:45.940
<v Ava>like going back to a theme you thought you knew.

274
00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:51.638
<v Ava>Hill climbing: always do whatever feels closest to passing right now. Rereading, highlighting,

275
00:23:51.638 --> 00:23:57.720
<v Ava>redoing the questions you can already answer, cramming the night before. Every step feels like progress,

276
00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:03.335
<v Ava>until you reach a local peak: you're fine on the questions you practised, and stuck on new ones.

277
00:24:03.675 --> 00:24:08.005
<v Leo>So means-ends costs more planning, but allows the detours you need.

278
00:24:09.305 --> 00:24:13.679
<v Leo>Block B. Sternberg. Lecture B point one. What's the core idea?

279
00:24:14.019 --> 00:24:19.361
<v Ava>Block A assumed a clear path. In a well-structured problem, like the Tower of Hanoi, the start,

280
00:24:19.361 --> 00:24:24.984
<v Ava>the goal and the allowed moves are all clear. It can still be hard, but you know where you're going.

281
00:24:25.184 --> 00:24:31.112
<v Ava>In an ill-structured problem, there's no clear path, and by definition no well-defined problem space.

282
00:24:31.112 --> 00:24:37.685
<v Ava>You don't even know which moves are possible. The difficulty is building a plan that inches toward the solution.

283
00:24:38.025 --> 00:24:40.927
<v Leo>And ill-structured doesn't simply mean very hard.

284
00:24:41.267 --> 00:24:48.456
<v Ava>Right. Four-disk Hanoi is hard but well-structured. As you'll see, an ill-structured problem can have a one-word answer.

285
00:24:49.756 --> 00:24:51.953
<v Leo>And the two-string problem is back.

286
00:24:52.293 --> 00:24:58.298
<v Ava>Yes, now from Sternberg's side. Two strings hang from the ceiling, too far apart to hold both at once,

287
00:24:58.298 --> 00:25:00.123
<v Ava>and you must tie them together.

288
00:25:00.323 --> 00:25:05.471
<v Ava>Sternberg says people go wrong at the step of defining the problem, and never recover.

289
00:25:05.471 --> 00:25:12.355
<v Ava>They define it as: move toward one string while holding the other. That self-made limit makes it almost impossible.

290
00:25:12.555 --> 00:25:18.471
<v Ava>So the solution needs insight: you have to restructure the problem. Not: how do I get to the string?

291
00:25:18.471 --> 00:25:23.264
<v Ava>But: how can the string come to me? We'll solve it fully in the Canvas questions.

292
00:25:24.564 --> 00:25:26.633
<v Leo>Sternberg gives three little puzzles.

293
00:25:26.973 --> 00:25:32.394
<v Ava>Haughty Harry has to build a hat rack from two sticks and a C-clamp. He fails. Sally succeeds,

294
00:25:32.394 --> 00:25:40.353
<v Ava>because she sees the clamp as a hat holder. Your text doesn't spell out the build, but the classic answer is to clamp the sticks together,

295
00:25:40.353 --> 00:25:44.275
<v Ava>wedge them between floor and ceiling, and hang the hat on the clamp.

296
00:25:44.475 --> 00:25:49.674
<v Ava>A woman married twenty men in her town. They're all alive, no divorces, no laws broken.

297
00:25:49.674 --> 00:25:53.499
<v Ava>She's a minister. She married them by performing their weddings.

298
00:25:53.699 --> 00:26:00.368
<v Ava>And the socks. Black and brown, five to one. How many to be sure of a pair? Three. Whatever the first two are,

299
00:26:00.368 --> 00:26:04.067
<v Ava>the third must match one of them. The ratio is a distraction.

300
00:26:04.407 --> 00:26:07.308
<v Leo>So each time, you have to redefine something.

301
00:26:07.648 --> 00:26:13.920
<v Ava>The materials, a word, or which information matters. That's insight: seeing the problem in a new way,

302
00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:16.032
<v Ava>restructuring your representation.

303
00:26:17.332 --> 00:26:19.252
<v Leo>So what exactly is insight?

304
00:26:19.592 --> 00:26:26.080
<v Ava>A distinctive, and sometimes seemingly sudden, understanding of a problem or of a strategy to solve it.

305
00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:31.560
<v Ava>Sternberg adds: it may feel sudden, but it's often the result of a lot of earlier work.

306
00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:37.051
<v Ava>The Gestalt psychologists studied it first. Köhler watched a chimpanzee called Sultan.

307
00:26:37.051 --> 00:26:47.141
<v Ava>Bananas hung out of reach. After jumping and stretching for them without success, Sultan suddenly realised he could stack the boxes in his enclosure and climb them.

308
00:26:47.341 --> 00:26:52.764
<v Ava>Wertheimer made a distinction: productive thinking goes beyond your existing associations,

309
00:26:52.764 --> 00:26:56.259
<v Ava>and reproductive thinking just uses what you already know.

310
00:26:56.459 --> 00:27:01.635
<v Ava>And the big Gestalt claim is that insight is a special process, different from normal,

311
00:27:01.635 --> 00:27:05.547
<v Ava>step-by-step processing. That's the claim the evidence now tests.

312
00:27:06.847 --> 00:27:11.860
<v Leo>Evidence. If insight is special, it should look different. Did Metcalfe find that?

313
00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:18.250
<v Ava>She did. While solving, people paused every fifteen seconds and rated how warm or cold they felt,

314
00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:20.371
<v Ava>meaning how close to the solution.

315
00:27:20.571 --> 00:27:27.099
<v Ava>For routine problems, algebra or the Tower of Hanoi, warmth went up step by step as people got closer.

316
00:27:27.299 --> 00:27:34.232
<v Ava>For insight problems, warmth stayed low, cold, cold, cold, and then jumped right before the solution appeared.

317
00:27:34.432 --> 00:27:41.280
<v Ava>And a second finding: people could predict whether they'd solve routine problems, but not insight problems.

318
00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:49.920
<v Ava>Successful solvers were often pessimistic, and unsuccessful ones optimistic. So this supports the Gestalt idea that insight is special.

319
00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:56.192
<v Ava>One honest note: Sternberg's figure is a set of histograms, and this line is a simplified version.

320
00:27:56.532 --> 00:28:01.887
<v Leo>Routine problems warm up slowly. Insight problems stay cold, then jump.

321
00:28:03.187 --> 00:28:05.661
<v Leo>But is insight really sudden and special?

322
00:28:06.001 --> 00:28:12.033
<v Ava>Let's look at the brain. During insight, activity rises in the right anterior temporal area,

323
00:28:12.033 --> 00:28:20.359
<v Ava>and EEG shows a burst of high-frequency activity. Right-hemisphere activity even shows up before the insight becomes conscious.

324
00:28:20.559 --> 00:28:26.765
<v Ava>The right hippocampus seems critical too. That makes sense, because insight combines stored information,

325
00:28:26.765 --> 00:28:29.391
<v Ava>and the hippocampus helps form new memories.

326
00:28:29.591 --> 00:28:35.607
<v Ava>And the most striking finding: brain activity before people even see a problem predicts insight.

327
00:28:35.607 --> 00:28:40.997
<v Ava>People who later solved with insight showed strong frontal activation while preparing.

328
00:28:40.997 --> 00:28:43.692
<v Ava>So insight may involve advance preparation.

329
00:28:43.892 --> 00:28:49.916
<v Ava>On the behaviour side, Smith argues that insights often come gradually, not as a sudden aha.

330
00:28:49.916 --> 00:28:52.404
<v Ava>That challenges the from-nowhere idea.

331
00:28:52.604 --> 00:28:58.919
<v Ava>And sleep increased the chance of insight in maths problems and in tasks that need you to discover hidden rules.

332
00:29:00.219 --> 00:29:01.883
<v Leo>Verdict on B point one?

333
00:29:02.223 --> 00:29:07.919
<v Ava>Ill-structured problems have no well-defined problem space, and the hard part is representing them.

334
00:29:08.119 --> 00:29:10.892
<v Ava>Insight means restructuring your representation.

335
00:29:11.092 --> 00:29:16.255
<v Ava>It feels different: no gradual warming up, then a jump. That supports the Gestalt view.

336
00:29:16.455 --> 00:29:21.755
<v Ava>But it isn't magic. It can be gradual, sleep helps, and the brain is preparing for it.

337
00:29:21.755 --> 00:29:25.884
<v Ava>And Sternberg warns that insights can be brilliant, and dead wrong.

338
00:29:26.224 --> 00:29:28.848
<v Leo>So: work hard first, then take a break.

339
00:29:30.148 --> 00:29:36.164
<v Leo>Canvas B point one. How is the aha experience related to Metcalfe's warmth research?

340
00:29:36.504 --> 00:29:43.688
<v Ava>Metcalfe makes the aha measurable. For insight problems, warmth ratings stay low until moments before the solution,

341
00:29:43.688 --> 00:29:52.184
<v Ava>and then jump. That sudden jump from cold to hot is the aha experience. Add that people could predict their success on routine problems,

342
00:29:52.184 --> 00:29:57.432
<v Ava>but not on insight problems. That supports the Gestalt view that insight is special.

343
00:29:57.632 --> 00:30:04.564
<v Ava>Then add two cautions. Metcalfe shows the feeling, not how insight works. And sudden doesn't mean from nowhere:

344
00:30:04.564 --> 00:30:11.371
<v Ava>insights often follow a lot of work, can come gradually, and brain activity during preparation predicts them.

345
00:30:12.671 --> 00:30:19.549
<v Leo>Canvas B point two. The two-string problem, with a book of matches, pliers and some cotton on the floor.

346
00:30:19.549 --> 00:30:23.913
<v Leo>What kind of problem is it, and how do people get to the solution?

347
00:30:24.253 --> 00:30:29.907
<v Ava>It's an ill-structured insight problem. There's no clear path, and you have to restructure how you see it.

348
00:30:30.107 --> 00:30:35.941
<v Ava>Two obstacles from the next lecture apply. Mental set: you fixate on moving yourself to the string.

349
00:30:35.941 --> 00:30:43.072
<v Ava>And functional fixedness: you see the pliers only as a tool. The solution is to tie the pliers to one string as a weight,

350
00:30:43.072 --> 00:30:48.965
<v Ava>and swing it like a pendulum. While it swings, you walk to the other string and catch the first one.

351
00:30:49.165 --> 00:30:55.464
<v Ava>How do you get there? Redefine the goal: how can the string come to me? Look at each object for other uses.

352
00:30:55.464 --> 00:31:00.703
<v Ava>Use your body: in Matlin, people who swung their arms solved the rope version more often.

353
00:31:00.703 --> 00:31:05.059
<v Ava>And if you're stuck after real effort, take an easy break, or sleep on it.

354
00:31:06.359 --> 00:31:12.737
<v Leo>And B point four. Critics say higher education focuses too much on well-structured problems.

355
00:31:12.737 --> 00:31:15.233
<v Leo>Do you agree, and what would you do?

356
00:31:15.573 --> 00:31:20.950
<v Ava>There's no single right answer, so give a reasoned position. For example: partly agree.

357
00:31:20.950 --> 00:31:29.416
<v Ava>Well-structured problems build domain knowledge, which helps with both kinds. But ill-structured problems also need justification skills,

358
00:31:29.416 --> 00:31:34.731
<v Ava>because you have to choose and defend a representation. Students rarely practise that.

359
00:31:34.931 --> 00:31:41.499
<v Ava>Proposal: open cases where students first define the problem. Comparing problems with the same structure,

360
00:31:41.499 --> 00:31:48.193
<v Ava>which built a schema in Gick and Holyoak. Asking why a solution works, not only how. Grading the reasoning.

361
00:31:48.193 --> 00:31:54.387
<v Ava>And building up gradually, which fits Pretz: beginners lack the knowledge to analyse open problems.

362
00:31:54.587 --> 00:32:01.264
<v Ava>Advantages: better preparation for real life, practice in defining and justifying, and less mental set.

363
00:32:01.464 --> 00:32:07.988
<v Ava>Disadvantages: grading is harder and more subjective, it takes more time, beginners may be overwhelmed,

364
00:32:07.988 --> 00:32:11.661
<v Ava>and vague answers can slip through without clear criteria.

365
00:32:12.961 --> 00:32:18.081
<v Leo>B point two: obstacles and aids. What's the logic that ties it together?

366
00:32:18.421 --> 00:32:23.200
<v Ava>Experience is a double-edged sword. Everything you've learned can help you, or block you.

367
00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:31.271
<v Ava>On the blocking side: mental set, also called entrenchment. You stick to a way of seeing or solving a problem that worked before,

368
00:32:31.271 --> 00:32:32.552
<v Ava>but doesn't work now.

369
00:32:32.752 --> 00:32:38.448
<v Ava>Functional fixedness: you can't see that an object can be used for something other than its usual use.

370
00:32:38.648 --> 00:32:44.404
<v Ava>And stereotypes: you see a pattern in a few cases and apply it to everyone. They harm the targets,

371
00:32:44.404 --> 00:32:48.397
<v Ava>and they also limit the problem solving of the people who hold them.

372
00:32:48.597 --> 00:32:53.809
<v Ava>On the helping side: positive transfer, where an old solution makes a new problem easier,

373
00:32:53.809 --> 00:32:56.853
<v Ava>as with analogies when you see the shared structure.

374
00:32:57.053 --> 00:33:01.448
<v Ava>And when you're stuck on the wrong path, incubation: a break can help you let go.

375
00:33:02.748 --> 00:33:07.612
<v Leo>Sternberg says similarity can mislead in two directions. Draw it for me.

376
00:33:07.952 --> 00:33:13.561
<v Ava>Think of two questions: is the content similar, and is the structure the same? If both match,

377
00:33:13.561 --> 00:33:17.360
<v Ava>transfer is easy. If neither does, there's nothing to transfer.

378
00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:22.965
<v Ava>The first trap: same structure, different content. The military and radiation problems.

379
00:33:22.965 --> 00:33:27.437
<v Ava>People miss the analogy, because the content hides the shared structure.

380
00:33:27.637 --> 00:33:33.395
<v Ava>The second trap is the opposite: similar content, different structure. That's transparency,

381
00:33:33.395 --> 00:33:39.596
<v Ava>seeing an analogy that isn't there. Studying for two psychology exams looks like the same problem,

382
00:33:39.596 --> 00:33:45.606
<v Ava>but a closed-book essay exam needs different strategies from an open-book multiple-choice exam.

383
00:33:45.606 --> 00:33:48.011
<v Ava>Transparency causes negative transfer.

384
00:33:48.351 --> 00:33:53.684
<v Leo>So what matters is how closely the systems of relationships match, not the content.

385
00:33:54.984 --> 00:33:57.971
<v Leo>Evidence for mental set. The water jars.

386
00:33:58.311 --> 00:34:04.261
<v Ava>You get three jars and must measure an exact amount. Here: A holds twenty-three, B forty-nine,

387
00:34:04.261 --> 00:34:06.097
<v Ava>C three, and you need twenty.

388
00:34:06.297 --> 00:34:12.598
<v Ava>Problems one to six could all be solved with the same rule: fill B, pour off A once and C twice.

389
00:34:12.598 --> 00:34:14.041
<v Ava>B minus A minus two C.

390
00:34:14.241 --> 00:34:21.303
<v Ava>But the later problems also have a much simpler solution. Here, just A minus C: twenty-three minus three is twenty.

391
00:34:21.503 --> 00:34:28.040
<v Ava>Between sixty-four and eighty-three percent of people who'd practised the first set kept using the long rule.

392
00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:33.257
<v Ava>Among controls with no practice, only one to five percent failed to use the simple one.

393
00:34:33.597 --> 00:34:37.117
<v Leo>So practice literally blinded them to the easy answer.

394
00:34:38.417 --> 00:34:39.996
<v Leo>And functional fixedness?

395
00:34:40.336 --> 00:34:47.611
<v Ava>Breaking it is often the whole solution. Breaking free of it let people use a reshaped coat hanger to get into a locked car.

396
00:34:47.811 --> 00:34:51.096
<v Ava>And let thieves open simple spring locks with a credit card.

397
00:34:51.296 --> 00:34:56.480
<v Ava>And it's the same step with the pliers as a pendulum weight, or the C-clamp as a hat hook.

398
00:34:56.680 --> 00:35:04.811
<v Ava>One trap for later: don't confuse this with fixation in Camarda's study. Functional fixedness is about an object's usual use.

399
00:35:04.811 --> 00:35:09.949
<v Ava>Camarda's fixation is about ideas: getting stuck on the most obvious solutions.

400
00:35:11.249 --> 00:35:16.327
<v Leo>Now positive transfer. Sternberg gives the numbers for the military story.

401
00:35:16.667 --> 00:35:21.979
<v Ava>With no story, or an irrelevant one, fewer than ten percent solved the radiation problem.

402
00:35:22.179 --> 00:35:27.299
<v Ava>When people memorised the military story as a story-recall experiment, thirty percent.

403
00:35:27.499 --> 00:35:35.499
<v Ava>When people had to work out the military solution themselves, about half did, and forty-one percent of those then solved the radiation problem.

404
00:35:35.699 --> 00:35:40.733
<v Ava>And with the military solution given plus a hint to use it, about seventy-five percent.

405
00:35:41.073 --> 00:35:43.164
<v Leo>So the hint makes the difference again.

406
00:35:43.504 --> 00:35:50.977
<v Ava>Yes. Similar domains help too, and physics students transferred better when they tried to understand why examples were solved,

407
00:35:50.977 --> 00:35:56.731
<v Ava>not just how. Sternberg's conclusion: we generally need to be looking for analogies to find them.

408
00:35:58.031 --> 00:36:00.271
<v Leo>And the last aid, incubation.

409
00:36:00.611 --> 00:36:05.776
<v Ava>You work on a problem, put it aside without consciously thinking about it, and come back.

410
00:36:05.776 --> 00:36:13.496
<v Ava>The idea is that the break helps you escape negative transfer, like a mental set, and maybe the problem is processed below awareness.

411
00:36:13.696 --> 00:36:21.120
<v Ava>Some researchers called incubation an essential stage of problem solving. Others found no experimental support for it.

412
00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:27.618
<v Ava>Sio and Ormerod's meta-analysis says: it depends. Incubation is more fruitful after longer preparation.

413
00:36:27.618 --> 00:36:34.404
<v Ava>Filling the break with a highly demanding task hurts. And divergent tasks, where you have to produce something,

414
00:36:34.404 --> 00:36:40.947
<v Ava>benefit more. Your excerpt stops mid-sentence there, so don't guess what they're compared with in the exam.

415
00:36:42.247 --> 00:36:43.911
<v Leo>Verdict on B point two?

416
00:36:44.251 --> 00:36:50.559
<v Ava>Experience cuts both ways. Mental set, functional fixedness and stereotypes block new solutions.

417
00:36:50.559 --> 00:36:55.749
<v Ava>Remember the water jars: sixty-four to eighty-three percent versus one to five.

418
00:36:55.949 --> 00:37:01.453
<v Ava>Positive transfer through analogy works best with a hint, two analogues, and asking why.

419
00:37:01.653 --> 00:37:06.475
<v Ava>Look for shared relationships, not shared content, and watch out for transparency.

420
00:37:06.675 --> 00:37:10.899
<v Ava>And incubation helps after real preparation, with an undemanding break.

421
00:37:11.239 --> 00:37:15.121
<v Leo>Set blinds, structure transfers, a quiet break unsticks.

422
00:37:16.421 --> 00:37:23.639
<v Leo>Canvas B point three: cars and swimming pools also cause deaths, and we don't shut down society for those,

423
00:37:23.639 --> 00:37:28.133
<v Leo>so we shouldn't for COVID either. What's wrong with this argument?

424
00:37:28.473 --> 00:37:34.629
<v Ava>It's transparency: an analogy based on surface similarity, causes deaths, rather than structure.

425
00:37:34.629 --> 00:37:39.887
<v Ava>And in an analogy, what matters is how closely the systems of relationships match.

426
00:37:40.087 --> 00:37:46.454
<v Ava>And the relationships differ. This part is my own analysis, not from the readings. A virus spreads,

427
00:37:46.454 --> 00:37:52.499
<v Ava>so one case causes more, while an accident doesn't. Many cases at once can overload hospitals.

428
00:37:52.499 --> 00:37:57.516
<v Ava>And an infected person endangers others, while swimming mainly risks yourself.

429
00:37:57.716 --> 00:38:03.810
<v Ava>Also, we do act on traffic and pool deaths, with measures that fit their structure, like speed limits,

430
00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:11.757
<v Ava>seat belts and lifeguards. So the analogy leads to negative transfer. Whether lockdowns were the right policy is a separate question.

431
00:38:11.757 --> 00:38:15.700
<v Ava>The point is only that this argument doesn't show they were wrong.

432
00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:24.502
<v Leo>Block C. Camarda and colleagues, 2018. Do we need inhibitory control to be creative? First,

433
00:38:24.502 --> 00:38:26.728
<v Leo>what is inhibitory control?

434
00:38:27.068 --> 00:38:31.356
<v Ava>Look at this word, RED, printed in red. Naming the ink colour is easy.

435
00:38:31.556 --> 00:38:37.481
<v Ava>Now RED printed in green. Name the ink colour. You have to stop yourself from reading the word.

436
00:38:37.481 --> 00:38:38.916
<v Ava>That's the Stroop task.

437
00:38:39.116 --> 00:38:45.658
<v Ava>Inhibitory control is the ability to stop a dominant, automatic response so you can give the right one.

438
00:38:45.658 --> 00:38:53.153
<v Ava>It's a core process in reasoning, decision-making and theory of mind. The question is whether creativity needs it too.

439
00:38:54.453 --> 00:38:56.885
<v Leo>And the creativity task is the egg.

440
00:38:57.225 --> 00:39:02.157
<v Ava>Make sure a hen's egg doesn't break when it's dropped from ten metres. Think for a second.

441
00:39:02.157 --> 00:39:03.199
<v Ava>What comes to mind?

442
00:39:03.539 --> 00:39:05.416
<v Leo>A parachute. Or a cushion.

443
00:39:05.756 --> 00:39:11.592
<v Ava>Exactly what most adults say. Their ideas fall into three categories: dampen the shock,

444
00:39:11.592 --> 00:39:17.964
<v Ava>protect the egg, or slow the fall. That's fixation: people follow the path of least resistance,

445
00:39:17.964 --> 00:39:19.708
<v Ava>the most accessible ideas.

446
00:39:19.908 --> 00:39:25.252
<v Ava>The original ideas are rarer: use a living thing, like training a bird to catch the egg,

447
00:39:25.252 --> 00:39:28.228
<v Ava>or change the egg itself, like freezing it first.

448
00:39:29.528 --> 00:39:32.259
<v Leo>So how does creativity relate to the brakes?

449
00:39:32.599 --> 00:39:38.740
<v Ava>There are two opposite views. The disinhibition view says creativity runs on automatic processes,

450
00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:44.375
<v Ava>loose associations. Your brakes get in the way, so if you weaken them, remote ideas flow.

451
00:39:44.575 --> 00:39:50.477
<v Ava>The inhibition view, the dual-process model of Cassotti and colleagues, says the opposite.

452
00:39:50.477 --> 00:39:56.511
<v Ava>Creative ideas take two steps. First, you inhibit the obvious ideas that System 1 hands you,

453
00:39:56.511 --> 00:40:01.364
<v Ava>the ones that cause fixation. Then you explore new concepts with System 2.

454
00:40:01.564 --> 00:40:07.495
<v Ava>So: System 1 offers a parachute, you put on the brake, and System 2 can then find freezing the egg.

455
00:40:07.835 --> 00:40:12.272
<v Leo>To have an original idea, you first have to stop the obvious one.

456
00:40:13.572 --> 00:40:15.833
<v Leo>What did the evidence say before this study?

457
00:40:16.173 --> 00:40:24.109
<v Ava>For disinhibition: some studies found negative correlations. People with better inhibition scored lower on divergent thinking.

458
00:40:24.309 --> 00:40:32.986
<v Ava>Patients with ADHD or bipolar disorder sometimes gave more original ideas, though they rarely had specific inhibition problems,

459
00:40:32.986 --> 00:40:35.445
<v Ava>and other clinical studies disagree.

460
00:40:35.645 --> 00:40:43.107
<v Ava>And Radel and colleagues first exhausted people's inhibitory resources, then asked for unusual uses of a brick.

461
00:40:43.107 --> 00:40:47.208
<v Ava>Depletion increased both the number and originality of ideas.

462
00:40:47.408 --> 00:40:52.677
<v Ava>For inhibition: Stroop performance correlated positively with creativity in adults.

463
00:40:52.877 --> 00:41:01.027
<v Ava>Industrial designers, who are experts at exploring alternatives, had both higher divergent thinking and better inhibitory control.

464
00:41:01.227 --> 00:41:07.568
<v Ava>And in the brain, activity in the inferior frontal gyrus, a region linked to inhibition,

465
00:41:07.568 --> 00:41:14.773
<v Ava>related to the originality of ideas. A meta-analysis of forty-five fMRI studies points the same way.

466
00:41:15.113 --> 00:41:16.905
<v Leo>But it's mostly correlations.

467
00:41:17.245 --> 00:41:24.755
<v Ava>Exactly. A correlation can't show that inhibition causes creativity. So Camarda's team manipulated it directly.

468
00:41:26.055 --> 00:41:28.081
<v Leo>How do you manipulate inhibition?

469
00:41:28.421 --> 00:41:34.873
<v Ava>With a dual task, which you know from Theme 2: if a second task uses up a resource the main task needs,

470
00:41:34.873 --> 00:41:36.251
<v Ava>the main task suffers.

471
00:41:36.451 --> 00:41:38.328
<v Ava>One group did the egg task alone.

472
00:41:38.528 --> 00:41:44.592
<v Ava>A control group did it while also doing a congruent Stroop, red in red ink. That needs attention,

473
00:41:44.592 --> 00:41:45.717
<v Ava>but no inhibition.

474
00:41:45.917 --> 00:41:51.208
<v Ava>And the key group did it with the incongruent Stroop, red in green ink, which keeps the brakes busy.

475
00:41:51.408 --> 00:41:57.355
<v Ava>Now the predictions are opposite. If disinhibition is right, the busy-brakes group should be more creative,

476
00:41:57.355 --> 00:42:00.133
<v Ava>because their brakes are effectively switched off.

477
00:42:00.333 --> 00:42:03.469
<v Ava>If the inhibition view is right, they should be less creative.

478
00:42:03.669 --> 00:42:09.941
<v Ava>And either way, the control group should match the single task, because it adds a task without loading inhibition.

479
00:42:11.241 --> 00:42:13.353
<v Leo>And how do you score creativity?

480
00:42:13.693 --> 00:42:16.445
<v Ava>Three ways. Fluidity: the number of solutions.

481
00:42:16.645 --> 00:42:20.635
<v Ava>Flexibility: how many different categories you use, out of fifty-four.

482
00:42:20.835 --> 00:42:28.379
<v Ava>And expansivity: how many solutions fall outside the three fixation categories. That's the measure of escaping the obvious,

483
00:42:28.379 --> 00:42:31.139
<v Ava>and it correlates highly with expert ratings.

484
00:42:31.339 --> 00:42:36.675
<v Ava>So a cushion, a box and a parachute give you three for fluidity, three for flexibility,

485
00:42:36.675 --> 00:42:38.208
<v Ava>and zero for expansivity.

486
00:42:39.508 --> 00:42:41.300
<v Leo>Results. Experiment one.

487
00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:48.213
<v Ava>Seventy-eight Paris undergraduates, five minutes, ideas spoken into a Dictaphone. Flexibility was dropped,

488
00:42:48.213 --> 00:42:53.608
<v Ava>because it correlated point eight two with fluidity: it measured almost the same thing.

489
00:42:53.808 --> 00:43:00.328
<v Ava>Fluidity: with busy brakes, about seven ideas, against about ten point seven with the egg task alone.

490
00:43:00.328 --> 00:43:06.718
<v Ava>That's an effect size d of point seven two, medium to large. And the control group, ten point four,

491
00:43:06.718 --> 00:43:08.784
<v Ava>was the same as the single task.

492
00:43:08.984 --> 00:43:14.685
<v Ava>Expansivity: one point seven three with busy brakes, against three point one two alone,

493
00:43:14.685 --> 00:43:22.680
<v Ava>d of point six one. The overall test was only marginal, p of point oh eight, but their planned comparison was significant.

494
00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.452
<v Leo>And the control group is the clever part.

495
00:43:25.792 --> 00:43:32.980
<v Ava>Yes. The congruent Stroop also adds a task and needs attention, yet it didn't hurt. So the cost comes from inhibition,

496
00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:35.051
<v Ava>not from doing two things at once.

497
00:43:36.351 --> 00:43:42.879
<v Leo>But maybe it's not inhibition specifically. Maybe any hard task costs executive resources.

498
00:43:43.219 --> 00:43:48.403
<v Ava>That's exactly why they ran Experiment two, with a new sample of seventy-nine students.

499
00:43:48.603 --> 00:43:55.259
<v Ava>This time the second task loaded working memory: remember the order of four dots in a four by four grid,

500
00:43:55.259 --> 00:44:00.507
<v Ava>then reproduce it. Complex patterns for high demand, a simple line for low demand.

501
00:44:00.707 --> 00:44:05.863
<v Ava>A separate control study checked that the high-demand version really was harder: slower,

502
00:44:05.863 --> 00:44:09.261
<v Ava>and less accurate, eighty-two versus ninety-three percent.

503
00:44:09.461 --> 00:44:16.176
<v Ava>And the result: no effect at all. Fluidity, eleven point oh eight versus ten point four five.

504
00:44:16.176 --> 00:44:20.725
<v Ava>Expansivity, five point two four versus five. Both F below one.

505
00:44:21.065 --> 00:44:24.777
<v Leo>Busy brakes, fewer ideas. Busy memory, no change.

506
00:44:26.077 --> 00:44:27.592
<v Leo>Verdict on Block C?

507
00:44:27.932 --> 00:44:34.716
<v Ava>Keeping the brakes busy cut both the number and the originality of ideas. Keeping working memory busy did not.

508
00:44:34.916 --> 00:44:40.377
<v Ava>So the cost is specific to inhibition, which is a more causal link than the earlier correlations.

509
00:44:40.577 --> 00:44:47.063
<v Ava>And neither experiment found that weaker brakes made people more creative, which goes against the disinhibition view.

510
00:44:47.263 --> 00:44:53.951
<v Ava>The limits: a null result in Experiment two isn't proof of no effect. The two experiments used different samples.

511
00:44:53.951 --> 00:44:59.632
<v Ava>The working-memory task might just have been too easy, though the authors find that less likely.

512
00:44:59.632 --> 00:45:06.143
<v Ava>And the Stroop uses words while the egg answers were spoken, so part of the cost could be verbal interference.

513
00:45:06.483 --> 00:45:08.445
<v Leo>Stop the obvious, then expand.

514
00:45:09.745 --> 00:45:17.703
<v Leo>Canvas C point one. Which two hypotheses are pitted against each other, and how would Figure 2 look under the alternative?

515
00:45:18.043 --> 00:45:23.882
<v Ava>The disinhibition hypothesis versus the inhibition hypothesis from the dual-process model.

516
00:45:23.882 --> 00:45:30.629
<v Ava>What we observed: the inhibition dual-task bar lowest, and the control and single-task bars about equal.

517
00:45:30.829 --> 00:45:38.333
<v Ava>Under disinhibition, the inhibition bar should be higher than the single task, because busy brakes would act like released brakes.

518
00:45:38.333 --> 00:45:41.219
<v Ava>The control bar would still equal the single task.

519
00:45:41.419 --> 00:45:47.170
<v Ava>And for Experiment two, a related view from Lin and Lien says working memory hurts creativity.

520
00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:51.637
<v Ava>Then the high-demand working-memory bar should be the highest. It wasn't.

521
00:45:52.937 --> 00:45:59.721
<v Leo>C point two. What's the difference between flexibility and expansivity, and why use several measures?

522
00:46:00.061 --> 00:46:07.835
<v Ava>Flexibility measures variety: how many of the fifty-four categories you use. Expansivity measures escaping the obvious:

523
00:46:07.835 --> 00:46:11.624
<v Ava>how many ideas fall outside the three fixation categories.

524
00:46:11.824 --> 00:46:17.472
<v Ava>Why several? Creativity has several aspects, and the hypotheses are about different ones.

525
00:46:17.472 --> 00:46:27.499
<v Ava>A drop in fluidity alone might just mean the second task slowed people down. Expansivity tests the specific claim that inhibition helps you get past fixation,

526
00:46:27.499 --> 00:46:29.403
<v Ava>and it matches expert ratings.

527
00:46:29.603 --> 00:46:36.858
<v Ava>And having several measures also showed that two of them overlapped: fluidity and flexibility correlated point eight two,

528
00:46:36.858 --> 00:46:38.477
<v Ava>so flexibility was dropped.

529
00:46:39.777 --> 00:46:44.897
<v Leo>C point three. Why was Experiment two run, and did it fully achieve its goal?

530
00:46:45.237 --> 00:46:52.939
<v Ava>Why: Experiment one couldn't tell whether the cost was specific to inhibition, or just a general cost of any demanding task.

531
00:46:53.139 --> 00:46:59.709
<v Ava>Largely achieved: working-memory load had no effect, and the control study showed the task really was harder.

532
00:46:59.909 --> 00:47:06.591
<v Ava>But not fully. A null result doesn't prove there's no effect. The dot task only requires storing information,

533
00:47:06.591 --> 00:47:11.924
<v Ava>so it may still have been too easy, which the authors admit. The samples differed. And,

534
00:47:11.924 --> 00:47:17.808
<v Ava>my own addition, the Stroop is verbal while the dot task is visual, and the answers were spoken.

535
00:47:18.008 --> 00:47:23.853
<v Ava>A stronger test would compare equally difficult inhibition and working-memory loads within one sample.

536
00:47:25.153 --> 00:47:32.791
<v Leo>And C point four: how does dual-process theory relate to creative idea generation, and to Experiment one?

537
00:47:33.131 --> 00:47:39.761
<v Ava>In general: the first ideas come from System 1, the dominant, accessible ones that cause fixation.

538
00:47:39.761 --> 00:47:47.541
<v Ava>To be creative, you inhibit them, and then System 2 explores new concepts, through conceptual expansion or analogy.

539
00:47:47.541 --> 00:47:50.517
<v Ava>So creativity needs control, not letting go.

540
00:47:50.717 --> 00:47:56.582
<v Ava>Experiment one fits exactly. With busy brakes, people couldn't suppress the System 1 ideas,

541
00:47:56.582 --> 00:48:02.704
<v Ava>so they produced fewer ideas and fewer original ones, while the congruent Stroop had no effect.

542
00:48:02.704 --> 00:48:06.120
<v Ava>And the rise that disinhibition predicted never came.

543
00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:12.435
<v Ava>And there's a nice link to Block A: the obvious egg answers are quick System 1 design heuristics,

544
00:48:12.435 --> 00:48:14.704
<v Ava>while analogy is slow System 2 work.

545
00:48:16.004 --> 00:48:17.924
<v Leo>Bring it all together for me.

546
00:48:18.264 --> 00:48:25.363
<v Ava>Block A: when there's a clear path, you represent the problem and choose a strategy. Analogy is the most powerful strategy,

547
00:48:25.363 --> 00:48:29.635
<v Ava>and the most fragile, because we see the surface instead of the structure.

548
00:48:29.835 --> 00:48:36.036
<v Ava>Block B takes the clear path away. You have to restructure, and experience itself gets in the way:

549
00:48:36.036 --> 00:48:39.200
<v Ava>mental set, functional fixedness, false analogies.

550
00:48:39.400 --> 00:48:48.083
<v Ava>And Block C shows the mechanism: escaping the obvious takes active inhibition, after which slower System 2 reasoning can find something new.

551
00:48:48.283 --> 00:48:54.074
<v Ava>So the theme makes one argument. Solving a problem depends less on effort than on representation,

552
00:48:54.074 --> 00:48:58.672
<v Ava>and the hardest step is often letting go of the first one that comes to mind.

553
00:48:59.972 --> 00:49:06.820
<v Leo>One more time, the memory hooks. Algorithm: always, but slowly. Heuristic: usually, but quickly.

554
00:49:07.160 --> 00:49:10.168
<v Ava>Notice, map, schema. Noticing is where it breaks.

555
00:49:10.508 --> 00:49:14.903
<v Leo>Means-ends plans the route. Hill climbing only looks at the next step.

556
00:49:15.243 --> 00:49:20.064
<v Ava>Routine problems warm up slowly. Insight problems stay cold, then jump.

557
00:49:20.404 --> 00:49:24.287
<v Leo>Set blinds, structure transfers, a quiet break unsticks.

558
00:49:24.627 --> 00:49:29.917
<v Ava>And: stop the obvious, then expand. Good luck with your studying, Sezen. When you get stuck,

559
00:49:29.917 --> 00:49:32.563
<v Ava>work hard on it first, then take a quiet walk.

560
00:49:32.903 --> 00:49:34.908
<v Leo>See you in the next deep dive.
